;; ============================================================
;; Profile selection and calculus dispatch  —  SPEC.md §5.1
;;
;; ⚠  M0, NOT YET IMPLEMENTED.
;;
;; The rungs differ only in their CALCULUS — the rule set plus its
;; normal form and saturation-state variant — while sharing the front
;; end, the fixpoint driver, and the output stage. A profile is
;; therefore a pluggable component behind a stable interface, and the
;; seam is built in v0 even with only `profile-el` behind it: a
;; dispatch point left in place is far cheaper than one retrofitted.
;;
;; SELECTION IS WHOLE-ONTOLOGY MEMBERSHIP IN A SINGLE IMPLEMENTED
;; PROFILE — NEVER A UNION. Pick the cheapest implemented profile that
;; contains EVERY construct in the ontology. If none does, the run is
;; partial, and WHICH profile it runs under is fixed by rule rather
;; than left open: choose the one leaving the fewest logical axioms
;; omitted, breaking ties by the fixed order el < horn-shiq < sroiq.
;; Without that rule two conforming implementations classify different
;; sub-theories of the same input and return different findings — both
;; sound, neither reproducible, and §6.8 violated ACROSS
;; IMPLEMENTATIONS rather than across runs.
;;
;; This matters because EL++ and Horn-SHIQ are INCOMPARABLE: an
;; ontology with both a role chain (v0, not v1) and an inverse (v1,
;; not v0) is inside neither, and "escalate when constructs span
;; fragments" would have an implementation pick one and silently drop
;; the other's constructs. Calling that escalation is wrong until a
;; rung actually CONTAINS the mixed language — which is v2's job.
;;
;; IN A v0-ONLY BUILD there is no rung to escalate to: an ontology
;; outside §5.2 has nothing above it, and the run is inconclusive —
;; never "coherent".
;;
;; THE GATE TESTS MEMBERSHIP IN HOWL'S IMPLEMENTED LANGUAGE, NOT IN
;; OWL 2 EL. The two are not the same set, and testing the wrong one is
;; precisely how an incomplete run gets reported as a complete one.
;;
;; Choosing a profile weaker than the ontology needs makes HOWL
;; incomplete for it and, critically for validation, able to MISS AN
;; INCOHERENCE. So an out-of-profile construct is never cosmetic: it
;; records the axiom in coverage.omitted, which no command may report
;; as coherent. It may still report INCOHERENT from that same run — a
;; derived contradiction is definitive whatever the coverage.
;; ============================================================

(module select
  (@doc "Syntactic profile detection and calculus dispatch (M0)")

  (import types (Profile ProfileSelection Omission ReasonerConfig))

  (export
    select-profile)

  (fn select-profile ((arena Arena) (selection ProfileSelection))
    (@intent "Resolve a ProfileSelection to the calculus that will run")
    (@spec ((Arena ProfileSelection) -> Profile))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; An EXPLICIT request is honoured exactly; `auto` resolves to the
    ;; cheapest implemented calculus, which in a v0-only build is the
    ;; only one. Pinned as a contract because the failure mode is
    ;; silent: a selector that quietly downgraded an explicit
    ;; `horn-shiq` request to `el` would classify a strictly smaller
    ;; theory than the caller asked for and report it as complete.
    (@post (match selection
             ((auto)       {$result == 'profile-el})
             ((explicit p) {$result == p})))
    ;; v0 has exactly one calculus wired in, so `auto` and any explicit
    ;; request both land on profile-el. The DISPATCH POINT is what
    ;; matters here, not the branching: when v1 lands, auto-selection
    ;; must start returning profile-horn-shiq for ontologies using
    ;; inverses or qualified number restrictions rather than reporting
    ;; inconclusive, and that change belongs in this function alone.
    (match selection
      ((auto)       'profile-el)
      ((explicit p) p))))
